[60.058559, "o", "…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me verify it can actually install a package:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did:\r\n\r\n**Problem:** The `pip` package was corrupted — the actual `pip/` package directory had been deleted from `/usr/local"]
[60.058565, "o", "/lib/python3.13/site-packages/`, but the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory remained. This made it look like pip was installed (metadata was there), but the code was gone, so every `pip` command crashed with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n**Fix:** I removed the stale `.dist-info` directory and re-bootstrapped pip using `python3 -m ensurepip --default-pip`, which restored the full pip package. Both `pip3` and `python3 -m pip` now work correctly.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=19 surface=a5d46c1799a3\r\n\u001b[?2004hroot@179df43e2bf8:/app# "]
